{"id":"76a34fcb-c75f-4c89-af6f-7d1ce9cbb960","object":"chat.completion","created":1785029377,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    word_token_indices = case[\\\"word_token_indices\\\"]\\n    word_labels = case[\\\"word_labels\\\"]\\n    token_count = case[\\\"token_count\\\"]\\n\\n    if len(word_token_indices) != len(word_labels):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Mismatch between number of word groups and labels\\\")\\n\\n    all_tokens = set()\\n    total_tokens = 0\\n    for group in word_token_indices:\\n        for idx in group:\\n            if not (0 <= idx < token_count):\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Token index {idx} out of range [0, {token_count-1}]\\\")\\n            if idx in all_tokens:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Duplicate token index {idx}\\\")\\n            all_tokens.add(idx)\\n            total_tokens += 1\\n\\n    if len(all_tokens) != token_count:\\n        raise ValueError(f\\\"Not all tokens covered: {len(all_tokens)} vs {token_count}\\\")\\n\\n    token_labels = [0] * token_count\\n    kept_indices = []\\n    for i, group in enumerate(word_token_indices):\\n        label = word_labels[i]\\n        for idx in group:\\n            token_labels[idx] = label\\n            if label == 1:\\n                kept_indices.append(idx)\\n\\n    return {\\\"kept_indices\\\": kept_indices, \\\"token_labels\\\": token_labels}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for NLP-LL2-01. The task: Project word-level keep/drop labels onto registered subword spans and recover a faithful ordered token mask. Input has word_token_indices (list of lists of token indices), word_labels (list of 0/1), token_count. Output must have kept_indices (list of integers) and token_labels (list of integers of length token_count, each 0 or 1). The mapping: For each word group (index i), its word_label is broadcast to all tokens in word_token_indices[i]. The token_labels array is built by assigning the label for each token based on which word group it belongs to. If a token is not in any word group? The problem says \"Reject overlapping, out-of-range, or uncovered token indices.\" So we must ensure that each token index from 0 to token_count-1 appears exactly once across all word groups? Actually \"uncovered\" means if a token index is not in any word group, that's a problem. But we need to return token_labels for all tokens, so we need to assign label 0 for tokens not covered? But the requirement \"no_unlabeled_tokens\" suggests all tokens must have a label. Possibly the input guarantees coverage? But the problem says \"Reject overlapping, out-of-range, or uncovered token indices.\" Means we should validate and if any such issue, maybe raise an error? However the output contract doesn't include error handling; maybe we can assume valid input. The fixture examples appear valid: token indices are contiguous 0..token_count-1 without gaps or overlaps. But we should still validate as per the hard contracts.\n\nImplement: \n- Extract word_token_indices, word_labels, token_count from case.\n- Validate lengths: len(word_token_indices) == len(word_labels). Each word group must have at least 1 token? Probably.\n- Validate token indices: all must be in [0, token_count-1], no overlap, and collectively cover all tokens from 0 to token_count-1. If not, we could raise ValueError, but the solve function should return a valid JSON object per schema. The schema requires kept_indices and token_labels. We can choose to handle by maybe returning empty arrays? But better to assume valid input as given. However the instructions say \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" So we should check and if invalid, perhaps return an empty or error? The output contract doesn't allow error fields. Possibly the test cases are valid. We'll implement validation and if fails, raise an exception; but the evaluator expects solve to not raise? It says \"must return a JSON-serializable object, must not mutate case, and may use only the Python standard library.\" It doesn't forbid raising exceptions, but the output contract expects a JSON object with kept_indices and token_labels. So if validation fails, the function might return some sentinel? But safer to assume valid.\n\nProcedure:\n- token_labels = [0]*token_count\n- kept_indices = []\n- For each word group i, label = word_labels[i]; for each token_idx in word_token_indices[i]: token_labels[token_idx] = label; if label == 1: append token_idx to kept_indices (but careful: if a token appears in multiple groups? Overlap not allowed. Also we must ensure we don't append duplicates if same token appears in multiple groups? But overlap invalid so it's fine.)\n- After assignment, ensure that all tokens from 0 to token_count-1 have been assigned a label (i.e., token_labels not 0? Actually label could be 0, so we need to check that token_labels were set, not just default 0. We can track a set of covered indices and compare with range(token_count).)\n- Then return {\"kept_indices\": sorted(kept_indices) ? The problem says \"Retain token indices whose broadcast label equals one.\" Order should be the order of tokens as they appear? The output schema doesn't specify order. Usually kept_indices should be in increasing order because tokens are ordered. The examples? Not provided. But likely ascending. The problem says \"recover a faithful ordered token mask\", so keep order of token indices. Since we iterate words in order and tokens in order within each word, the kept_indices will be in the order they appear. But if multiple words have label 1, the indices from earlier words come first. That should be fine. But to be safe, we can sort, but the spec doesn't say sorted. I'll keep insertion order (which is the natural order as we iterate words in order and tokens in order within each word). That yields indices in increasing order because tokens are increasing within each word and words are in order of token indices (non-decreasing). So it's sorted.\n\nEdge cases: token_count might be larger than the sum of indices? But we validate. Also word_labels may contain only 0/1.\n\nImplement solve(case):\n- case is a dict with keys: word_token_indices, word_labels, token_count.\n- Validate that word_token_indices is a list of lists; word_labels list of ints; token_count int.\n- Check lengths match.\n- Create a set of all token indices. Check no duplicates: use set of tokens and compare total count; also check each index in range.\n- Then assign labels.\n- Verify that the set of covered indices equals set(range(token_count)). If not, maybe raise ValueError.\n- Then build result.\n- Return dict.\n\nWe'll write Python code.\n\nNow produce the JSON with \"implementation\" key containing the escaped Python source.\n\nBe careful with JSON escaping: newlines and backslashes and quotes.\n\nThe source code should be a single string.\n\nWrite the function.\n\nLet me draft:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    word_token_indices = case[\"word_token_indices\"]\n    word_labels = case[\"word_labels\"]\n    token_count = case[\"token_count\"]\n\n    if len(word_token_indices) != len(word_labels):\n        raise ValueError(\"Mismatch between number of word groups and labels\")\n\n    # Validate token indices\n    all_tokens = set()\n    total_tokens = 0\n    for group in word_token_indices:\n        for idx in group:\n            if not (0 <= idx < token_count):\n                raise ValueError(f\"Token index {idx} out of range [0, {token_count-1}]\")\n            if idx in all_tokens:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Duplicate token index {idx}\")\n            all_tokens.add(idx)\n            total_tokens += 1\n\n    if len(all_tokens) != token_count:\n        raise ValueError(f\"Not all tokens covered: {len(all_tokens)} vs {token_count}\")\n\n    # Build token labels\n    token_labels = [0] * token_count\n    kept_indices = []\n    for i, group in enumerate(word_token_indices):\n        label = word_labels[i]\n        for idx in group:\n            token_labels[idx] = label\n            if label == 1:\n                kept_indices.append(idx)\n\n    # At this point, all tokens have been assigned a label (0 or 1)\n    # No need to check uncovered because we validated coverage.\n\n    return {\"kept_indices\": kept_indices, \"token_labels\": token_labels}\n```\n\nNote: We assume the input is valid. But we can also sort kept_indices just in case? Since we iterate groups in order and tokens within group are increasing, kept_indices will be in increasing order. So fine.\n\nNow encode as JSON string. We'll write the implementation value as a string.\n\nThe output JSON should be {\"implementation\": \"<escaped code>\"}.\n\nWe must ensure no backticks or extra text.\n\nI'll produce the final JSON."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1143,"completion_tokens":1958,"total_tokens":3101,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1024},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1623},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1024,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":119},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}